{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"Compare **all-in, risk-adjusted costs and control**, not just the platform fee.\n\nA series trust can avoid much of the startup burden—forming and maintaining a trust, independent-board governance, compliance and vendor coordination—and may bring established servicing relationships and launch processes. For a sponsor with one ordinary ETF and limited fixed-cost capacity, those savings can outweigh recurring platform, administration, custody, and other pass-through charges. Check fee minimums, termination/transition costs, service levels, conflicts, and who controls naming, policies, vendors, and launch timing.\n\nBuilding a standalone trust typically gives the sponsor more direct control over governance, operations, branding, and future product expansion, but requires funding and managing the infrastructure and oversight itself. The fixed costs may be hard to justify for one fund; they can make more sense if the sponsor expects scale, values independence, or needs features the platform will not support.\n\nCompare proposals with counsel and service providers; confirm current legal and operational requirements."}}
